# mux config.sample - every knob $MUX_DIR/config accepts, with its default.
#
# COPY WHAT YOU NEED; DO NOT COPY THE FILE. Every line below is commented
# out and shows what mux already does, so an uncommented copy of this file
# changes nothing today and quietly freezes those values at whatever they
# were the day you copied it.
#
# THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE RULE HERE, and this package learned it the
# expensive way. A config once restated three latch seams that were already
# mux's own defaults, and one of them restated it WRONG (it omitted
# ServerAliveCountMax=3, which mux sets explicitly); a fourth named a hook
# file a later release had moved, and `mux latch` broke on both machines the
# day it did. An ACTIVE line is a second copy of a fact that can drift. A
# COMMENTED one cannot: nothing reads it, so the worst it can do is go out of
# date as documentation, which is why this file is shipped BESIDE the code
# and is held against it by `test/mux-config-sample.t` rather than by anyone
# remembering.
#
# SO: set a key here only to OVERRIDE mux, and leave the rest commented.
#
# THE FORMAT is one directive per line, `KEY VALUE`, with VALUE running to
# end of line. A `#` that FOLLOWS WHITESPACE starts a comment and is removed;
# one inside a token is kept, so `bg=#3f5f00` survives. A trailing backslash
# is NOT a line join: a wrapped directive silently becomes a truncated one,
# so every value must fit on one line.
#
# A BARE NAME in any hook-shaped value resolves against $MUX_DIR first, then
# $MUX_SHARE, then $PATH, so this file travels between machines without
# carrying an absolute path that is right on only one of them.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE: anything that can differ per context or per partition.
# Those live in partitions/<name>.partition and contexts/<token>.context,
# and this file is for the invariants only. See mux(1) FILES.


# --- CONTEXT ---------------------------------------------------------------

# context-command COMMAND...
#
# The one command mux asks for the current context TOKEN, printed as a single
# word on stdout. Empty output or a non-zero exit means `global`, the
# baseline, so a box with no integration is a box with one partition. Whatever
# backs a boundary (a group, an ACL, a namespace) lives in the integrator;
# mux only reflects what this answers.
#
# Default: unset, which is `global`.
#context-command   myctx current


# --- SESSION ENVIRONMENT ---------------------------------------------------

# env-ready NAME...
#
# The session pointers this machine cannot usefully work without. When
# `mux resume` finds no server for a partition (so this host has not been set
# up since it came up) and one of these has not arrived, it exits 4 instead
# of rebuilding every session into an environment that is still starting. A
# process environment is fixed at exec, so panes built in that window can
# never gain the value afterwards. A latch retries and each attempt re-reads
# the environment, so the wait needs nothing else.
#
# Each name is checked by running its OWN hook in envhooks.d, so there is no
# second copy of the liveness question. A name nothing manages is a typo, and
# `mux check` reports it rather than leaving you a wait that never ends.
#
# DECLARED, NEVER DETECTED: a box that will never have a compositor and one
# whose compositor has not started yet look identical from here, and the
# second is the whole reason this exists.
#
# Default: empty, so nothing waits.
#env-ready   WAYLAND_DISPLAY WAYFIRE_SOCKET

# env-timeout SECONDS
#
# How long one envhooks.d validator may take before its answer is treated as
# undetermined. Long enough for a connect to a local socket, short enough
# that a wedged hook is not mistaken for a wedged mux. A non-numeric value is
# ignored rather than passed to timeout(1), because a typo must not silently
# disable validation.
#
# Default: 2
#env-timeout   2


# --- LATCH -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Five seams, each also settable as MUX_LATCH_<NAME> in the environment,
# which wins over this file. mux owns the state machine; the transport is
# yours. See mux(1) LATCH.
#
# `none` AS THE VALUE TURNS A SEAM OFF, on any of them. An EMPTY value does
# NOT: it reads as absent and falls through to the default, so there has to be
# a positive spelling for "I do not want one".

# latch-transport TEMPLATE
#
# How to reach the far side. `%h` is the host, `%p:DEFAULT` the port with its
# fallback, `%c` the remote command as ONE argv element and `%q` the same
# thing shell-quoted. ssh needs `%q` because it concatenates its arguments
# and the remote shell re-splits them; `docker exec` and Eternal Terminal
# need `%c`. The `sh -lc` is load-bearing for ssh and only for ssh: sshd runs
# a remote command without a login shell, so without it PATH has no ~/.local.
#
# Default:
#latch-transport   ssh -t -o ServerAliveInterval=5 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 -o ConnectTimeout=10 -p %p:22 %h sh -lc %q

# latch-auth HOOK
#
# Asked whether a credential is available before an attempt, so a refusal
# becomes a WAIT rather than a prompt storm. Exit 0 live, 1 not, 78 cannot
# tell.
#
# Default: ssh-auth
#latch-auth   ssh-auth

# latch-classify HOOK
#
# Turns a transport's exit code and stderr into one state word. ssh exits 255
# for auth-denied, unreachable and unresolvable alike, so stderr is the only
# discriminator, and that specificity is exactly why it is a seam.
#
# Default: ssh-classify
#latch-classify   ssh-classify

# latch-probe HOOK
#
# A cheap question asked BEFORE each attempt. `ssh-probe` traverses the same
# auth path under timeout(1), so it catches the one hang no ssh option can
# bound: a RESPONSIVE peer making no progress, where ConnectTimeout is
# satisfied by the banner and every ServerAlive probe is answered. Measured on
# the author's fleet at 342s, once 5m42s, with nothing on screen.
#
# `none` TURNS IT OFF, and no probe means no opinion: latch attempts, and the
# attempt is the probe. An EMPTY value is not an off switch, it reads as
# absent and falls through to the default. Cost of leaving it on: 0.57s
# reachable, 5.03s blackholed, 0.03s unresolvable, once per attempt, which on
# a healthy link is once per attach.
#
# A DIFFERENT TRANSPORT NEEDS A DIFFERENT PROBE: the hook is handed the
# target's PORT, so on an Eternal Terminal latch this would test SSH on
# etserver's port and park latch in a backoff about a reachable host. Set
# `latch-probe et-probe` there.
#
# Default: ssh-probe
#latch-probe   ssh-probe

# latch-status HOOK
#
# Run on every state change with the state and its detail, for a bar or an
# external indicator. Unset means nobody asked.
#
# Default: unset.
#latch-status   my-latch-status

# latch-restore HOOK
#
# Repairs the terminal after a transport dies under a live attach. tmux never
# sends its teardown in that case, so the terminal is left with the cursor
# hidden, mouse reporting on and the alternate screen up. `stty sane` fixes
# only the half the kernel owns.
#
# Default: mux-sane, mux's own helper.
#latch-restore   mux-sane

# latch-backoff-rung N
#
# Attempts at each delay before doubling it (2s, 4s, 8s ... 60s). Pure
# doubling reaches the cap on the sixth wait, about a minute in, so a box
# that reboots and answers again at 90s costs you a full minute of waiting
# for a host that has been up for most of it. Raise it to be more patient,
# lower it to climb faster.
#
# Default: 5
#latch-backoff-rung   5

# latch-et-port PORT
#
# Which port `et-probe` tests when the target did not name one. Prefer the
# target (`mux latch box:2022`), which tells the transport and the probe the
# same thing; this key exists for a target written without one. Also
# MUX_ET_PORT.
#
# Default: 2022
#latch-et-port   2022


# --- DESKTOP NOTIFIER ------------------------------------------------------
# Read by the optional `mux-desktop-notifier` daemon, not by mux core. Each
# is also MUX_DESKTOP_NOTIFIER_<NAME> in the environment, which wins.

# desktop-notifier-transport TEMPLATE
#
# How the daemon reaches a latched host to stream its agent state. Same
# tokens as latch-transport. BatchMode is deliberate: a daemon must never
# block on a prompt nobody is there to answer.
#
# Default:
#desktop-notifier-transport   ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=6 -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new -p %p:22 %h %q

# desktop-notifier-ignore NAME...
#
# Partitions the daemon draws no tray item and raises no banner for. `mux
# demo` runs four pretend agents in a loop, so its namespace is ignored by
# default; name it here to watch the demo, or add a real partition you would
# rather not see.
#
# Default: mux.demo
#desktop-notifier-ignore   mux.demo

# desktop-notifier-toast HOOK
#
# Compose the banner yourself. The hook is handed
# `KIND SESSION HOST PARTITION LOCALITY` and prints the SUMMARY on its first
# line and the BODY on every line after it; mux falls back to its own wording
# on any failure, so a mistake costs styling and never the notification.
#
# WHY IT IS A SEAM: which field a notification daemon renders, and whether it
# interprets markup in it, is the DAEMON's choice. mako reads Pango in the
# body and not in the summary; dunst differs; a macOS presenter has no Pango
# at all. mux emits text that reads correctly everywhere and leaves styling
# to you. `toast-pango` in $MUX_SHARE/desktop-notifier/ is a worked example
# (a dim, normal-weight host on the title row) and documents the one line of
# daemon config it REQUIRES: without that line its empty host row renders as a
# blank one. So the question that picks a hook is whether your daemon's format
# is configurable (mako and dunst, yes; swaync, GNOME Shell, plasma and
# xfce4-notifyd, no), and where it is not the answer is to leave this unset.
#
# Default: unset, so the wording is mux's own and carries no markup.
#desktop-notifier-toast   toast-pango

# desktop-notifier-activate HOOK
#
# Run locally after a tray click has switched that host's client, handed the
# item's LABEL. Clicking switches the session; RAISING the window that shows
# it means knowing about a compositor, and mux manages sessions inside
# terminals with no opinion about where a terminal sits. Samples in
# $MUX_SHARE/desktop-notifier/.
#
# Default: unset, so the click does only the part mux owns.
#desktop-notifier-activate   focus-kitty
